WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:01.730
- Hey, legal eagles.

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:03.850
It's no secret that law
school is expensive.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:05.800
It takes three years of your life,

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.520
it's stressful, but it's also rewarding,

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.840
and it's the gateway to becoming a lawyer.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.360
But the question is, is it worth it?

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:14.870
Well, today we're going to explore

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.720
whether it's the right decision for you

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.610
and whether it's worth the
huge upfront investment.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:20.543
So stick around.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.700
So the costs of law school are well known

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.060
and they are very large.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.490
Average tuition alone is
more than $40,000 per year.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:36.400
And when you add in living expenses,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.990
you're basically guaranteed to spend

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:41.000
over $50,000 per year for three years

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.080
unless you get a very
generous financial aid package

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.450
from your school.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.350
The average law school graduate in 2015

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:52.460
had over $140,000 in student debt

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.740
to obtain his or her law degree.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.530
And those numbers are
increasing every year

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:00.480
at about four to six times
the rate of inflation.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.420
Obviously, things get
more expensive over time,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.690
but law school is getting more expensive

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.860
a lot faster than almost everything else.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.920
Law school debt is often
at high interest rates

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.059
like 7 or 8%.

00:01:14.059 --> 00:01:17.420
And for perspective, if
you had $150,000 in debt

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.540
at 8% amortized over 10 years,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:24.430
you'd have to pay back
about $30,000 per year.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.560
And of course, you have to
pay that using after-tax money

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.900
because there are very few tax incentives

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.720
for paying back law school loans.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.370
So when you look at the
average salary of an attorney,

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.360
you should subtract about $30,000 per year

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.270
in after-tax money from the salary

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.680
because that's the contribution
that you have to make

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.390
for law school loans.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:47.860
But that's not the only cost.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.260
There's also the opportunity cost.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.890
It's not just the sticker
price of law schools.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:56.130
It's also the money you would
have earned somewhere else

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.970
if you had not gone to law school.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.380
For example, median
household income in the US

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.220
is about $60,000,

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.870
and if you're the type of person

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.280
who can get into a reasonable law school,

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.310
odds are you have the potential to earn

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:12.950
over $60,000 per year

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.160
after you graduate from college.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.510
So over the three years

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:19.120
that you would have gone to law school,

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.160
you could have made about
$180,000 on average.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.900
And that's money that you would not make

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.920
and you would have to forego
if you go to law school.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.620
Now, for context,

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.930
if you're able to get a big firm job,

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.770
they generally start, in big cities,

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:40.140
paying between $165k and $190,000 per year

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.270
plus often a bonus if you
work a number of hours.

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:47.670
And within three years of
working as a big firm associate,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:51.230
you can make well over $200,000.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.230
But there's often a huge price to pay

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.500
in order to get those big pay cheques.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.560
Big firms are often located
in the most expensive cities,

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.500
which means you're going to be paying

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.280
the most in living expenses

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:05.600
in order to make that big pay cheque.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.270
And of course, only top
graduates from top law schools

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.520
get those jobs.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.230
We did another video on
the prospects of getting

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.140
a top law firm job.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.210
The link to that will be
down in the description.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:19.500
So check that out to
see what your odds are

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.530
based on what law school you go to

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:22.820
and where you have to graduate

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.310
in those particular law schools.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:26.670
But that being said,

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.570
only a small portion
of law school graduates

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.340
actually get a big firm
job when they graduate.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.870
According to the New York Times, in 2015,

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:38.120
more than 20% of law school graduates

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.180
held jobs that didn't
even require a law degree.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:43.870
So imagine going to law
school for three years

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:45.740
and then not even
practicing as an attorney.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:49.710
Well, 20% of graduates don't
even practice as attorneys.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:54.480
And at the same time, in 2015,
only 40% worked in law firms

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.070
compared to 60% from the class of 2000.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:00.700
So, the odds of getting a big firm job

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:04.150
or even a medium or small-sized firm job

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:07.000
are much lower now than
they were 10 years ago.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.580
So keep that in mind
when you're factoring in

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.930
whether law school is
worth the investment.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.610
So I know it's really easy to focus on

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.810
the top law schools and the top law firms

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:20.550
to see what kind of numbers
people make when they graduate.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:21.950
And it's easy to fixate

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:25.290
on people making close to $200,000

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.530
right when they graduate from law school.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:30.420
But those kind of numbers
can really skew things.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.240
In 2015, median pay for
law school graduates

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:35.520
across the board

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.410
was only $64,800.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:42.240
So that's a bump against
the median US salary

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.970
of only about $5,000 per year.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.390
So looking at averages only,

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:51.130
many people are paying close
to $200,000 for law school

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:55.080
for the opportunity to only
make $5,000 more per year.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.500
So given the debt and the opportunity cost

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.970
and the upfront cost
of going to law school,

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:02.270
is it worth it?

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:03.870
Well, it depends.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:04.830
It can be.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.740
It certainly was for me.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.380
But it's also not for everyone.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:10.410
Here's generally how I think about it.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:14.180
If you know you have to be a lawyer,

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.820
you should probably go to law school.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:18.640
If you know deep down in
your heart, in your bones

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.680
that no job is for you
except being a lawyer,

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.500
then law school might be a good choice.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.340
If you aren't sure whether you
want to be a lawyer or not,

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:27.870
do not go to law school.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.900
Full stop, end of discussion, that's it.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.730
It's not worth it, you
will have a terrible time,

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:36.100
and it's not worth the personal cost

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.780
and the monetary cost to go to law school.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:42.380
Law school is a necessary
step to become a lawyer.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:45.730
And any ancillary benefits
like learning how to argue

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:50.220
could be learned better, faster,
and cheaper somewhere else.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.410
But if you won't be happy
unless you are a lawyer,

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:55.453
then law school is probably for you.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.300
Law school could be for you

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:01.490
if you are prepared for
a huge debt liability.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:02.900
Those are some big numbers.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:03.733
It can be manageable

00:06:03.733 --> 00:06:05.800
when you're paying it off over 10 years.

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:08.910
I would not count on
a public interest fund

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.700
where they pay off your loans.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.920
That can require you to pay off your loans

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:16.270
over a 20 or 30-year timescale,

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:20.370
which can add tons of costs and fees.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:23.690
So I would assume that I was
going to pay off the debt

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:27.200
over a 10-year period at
roughly a 7 or 8% interest.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:29.640
If you're prepared for that kind of debt,

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.253
then law school might be
a good decision for you.

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:35.360
If you can get lots of scholarships,

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.960
law school might be a good choice for you.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.410
If you can avoid the upfront cost

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:42.923
of the published rate of law school,

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.130
then that changes the metric
of whether it's worth it

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:48.653
to incur the opportunity
cost of law school.

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:51.620
If you are confident that you can be

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:54.000
in the top 10% of your class.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.430
Now, law school is kind
of like Lake Wobegon

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.130
where everyone thinks
they're above average.

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:02.300
But if you are 100% sure

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:03.740
that you can kick ass in law school,

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:06.570
and especially if you're
going to a top law school,

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:08.130
then odds are you can be

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:10.280
on the right hand side of the distribution

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.380
and you can get one of
those jobs that pays

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:14.650
well over the median salary,

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:15.600
and you stand a good chance

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.340
of being able to pay off
your debt in the future.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:22.030
Also, if you are prepared
to work 3,000 hours per year

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:23.340
at a big firm,

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:25.910
that can also sway things in your favor

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:29.030
to make law school a good decision.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.890
If you're prepared to do
the kind of big firm work,

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:33.790
then it might be a good stepping stone

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.593
to getting in to that big firm job.

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:38.860
If none of those things describe you,

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.170
consider law school very carefully.

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:44.920
You can't discharge the debt
of law school in bankruptcy.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:46.470
So it's going to stay with you,

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:47.863
basically, forever.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.250
I know that this may sound like I'm

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:52.970
talking down about law school.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:55.370
I had a great time in law school.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:57.000
I thought it was a wonderful experience,

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.240
and contrary to popular
belief, I had a ton of fun.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.910
I really loved it.

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:03.000
But at the same time,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.110
I recognize that it's not for everyone.

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:08.220
For some people, it's not
worth it to incur that debt.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.023
And some people really regret it.

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:13.490
I was lucky that I'm
not one of those people.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:16.160
I don't regret my decision
to go to law school.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:17.900
I certainly don't regret my decision

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:20.460
to become a practicing trial lawyer.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:21.530
But it's not for everyone,

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.490
and you need to decide for yourself,

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:27.130
and hopefully, this will
help you make that decision.

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:30.230
But if you have decided
that law school is for you,

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:33.300
be sure to download our free
ultimate pre-law checklist.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:34.570
It's our free gift to you,

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:35.960
and it will give you a headstart

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:38.490
by the time that you get to law school.

